{
  "summary": "This computer needs your attention soon. In the last 7 days, three things changed together: Defender's real-time protection was switched off, a new account (<account-3>) was created and given full administrator control, and a new automatic task called CacheCleaner appeared that runs a program from a temporary folder. Each can have an innocent explanation, but together they match a pattern often seen after a break-in. First, confirm whether you or someone you trust made these changes, starting with Defender and the new administrator account.",
  "groups": [
    {
      "priority": 1,
      "title": "Defender real-time protection was turned off since the last scan",
      "finding_ids": [
        "DEF-REALTIME-OFF:6ed445bc",
        "SETTING-WEAKENED:916db687"
      ],
      "explanation": "Microsoft Defender is installed, running and up to date, but its real-time protection is off. That means it won't block harmful files as they arrive. The earlier scan 7 days ago showed it on, so it was switched off recently. Turning off protection is often one of the first things malware does. If you didn't turn it off yourself, treat this as urgent. The fix shown in the report for these findings explains how to turn it back on."
    },
    {
      "priority": 2,
      "title": "New account <account-3> was created and made an administrator",
      "finding_ids": [
        "NEW-ADMIN:29255107",
        "NEW-USER:8187239c",
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:feb6df5d"
      ],
      "explanation": "These three findings are about the same account, <computer>\\<account-3>. It didn't exist at the last scan. It is now enabled and has full administrator control, so it can change anything on this computer. If you or another household member set it up, this may be fine. If nobody recognizes it, it could be a way for someone else to keep getting in, especially since Defender was weakened in the same period. See the report's fix for these findings."
    },
    {
      "priority": 3,
      "title": "New automatic task 'CacheCleaner' runs a program from a temporary folder",
      "finding_ids": [
        "NEW-PERSISTENCE:79b21359",
        "PERSIST-REVIEW:658daf42"
      ],
      "explanation": "Both findings are about one scheduled task named CacheCleaner. It was added since the last scan and runs a program called cc.exe from your user's Temp folder. Programs can write to that folder freely, and it's an unusual place for a real app to start from. It runs with limited rights under your user account, not as administrator. If you don't recognize it as part of something you installed, look at it closely; the report shows how to review or remove it."
    },
    {
      "priority": 4,
      "title": "Check you recognize the administrator account <account-2>",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:fde0b291"
      ],
      "explanation": "<computer>\\<account-2> is another account with full administrator control. It was already there before the last scan, so it isn't a new change. Having a separate administrator account is often good practice. Just confirm it's one you or someone you trust created."
    }
  ],
  "severity_notes": [
    {
      "finding_id": "NEW-PERSISTENCE:79b21359",
      "suggested_severity": "high",
      "reason": "The task runs a program straight from a Temp folder, and it appeared in the same period that Defender was switched off and a new administrator was added. Together these make it more concerning than a typical new startup entry."
    }
  ]
}
